Research title: "Comparative Analysis of Successful and Failed Enterprise System Implementations"


Case Study 1: Successful Enterprise System Implementation by N&N Moving Supplies

    Company Overview
        N&N Moving Supplies, a family-owned distributor of moving equipment, expanded from one to three locations, thereby nearly tripling its headcount. Initially, QuickBooks and a third-party payroll provider sufficed but proved insufficient for time record tracking and reconciliation of payroll with financial accounts. N&N decided to establish NetSuite ERP as a means to enhance efficiency and streamline processes.

    Implementation Process
        To ensure a smooth transition, N&N partnered with NOVAtime to integrate a time-clock solution within NetSuite ERP. This integration aimed to automate payroll, enhance financial tracking, and provide employees with access to personalized dashboards via iPads at each site. The company prioritized user training and change management to maximize adoption.

    Key Success Factors

        • Employee Buy-in: Personalized dashboards and proactive communication ensured user adoption.
        • Strategic Partnerships: Collaboration with NOVAtime helped tailor the ERP system to N&N’s needs.
        • Process Automation: Automated payroll and financial tracking reduced errors and administrative workload.
        • Cross-location Visibility: Real-time labor cost tracking improved financial decision-making.

    Outcomes & Impact

        • 84% Reduction in Payroll Processing Time: Automation streamlined calculations and approvals.
        • Faster Account Balancing & Improved Accuracy: Enhanced reconciliation of financial records.
        • Enhanced Workforce Management: Employees gained real-time access to work hours, leave balances, and performance metrics.
        • Data-driven Decision-Making: Insights into labor cost trends supported better financial planning and resource allocation.


Case Study 2: Failed Enterprise System Implementation by Nike

    Company Overview
        Nike is the sportswear leader around the world. In 2000, Nike initiated an upgrade of ERP that was valued at $400 million. It wanted to simplify the processes in its supply chain and order fulfillment. The intention was to enhance inventory management, better demand forecasting, and integrating the business functions. However, this led to extreme disruption and the company lost sales of $100 million with the stock price declining by 20%.

    Implementation Process & Challenges
        Nike implemented a new ERP system and did not synchronize business processes to meet operational requirements. The firm did not run any tests of the system prior to deploying the system. Hence, there existed inventory mismatches and fulfillment errors, particularly regarding high-demand Air Jordan footwear products. Unrealistic goals and a very aggressive schedule only exacerbated matters.

    Key Failure Factors

        • Unrealistic Expectations: Nike overestimated the immediate effects of the system and implemented it too quickly.
        • Lack of Testing: Inadequate testing of the system resulted in operational breakdowns and wrong orders.
        • Process Misalignment: The business processes of Nike did not match well with the ERP system.
        • Poor Change Management: Workers were not sufficiently prepared; it resulted in much confusion and waste.

    Outcomes & Consequences

        • $100 Million in Lost Sales: System failures led to stock shortages and unfulfilled orders.
        • 20% Stock Price Drop: Investor confidence declined due to operational setbacks.
        • Reputational Damage: The failed implementation impacted customer trust and brand image.
        • Costly Recovery Efforts: Nike had to invest additional resources to rectify the system flaws and restore normal operations.


